## Project 1: Low-cost behavioral imaging and robotics platform

Students will help design and build a low-cost, beginner-friendly imaging and robotics rig using Raspberry Pi hardware and Python-based control systems. This system will enable automated time-lapse behavioral recording of *Nematostella vectensis*, allowing us to quantify movement patterns, feeding behavior, and responses to environmental change.

Key skills include:
- Basic hardware assembly and experimental setup  
- Python programming for data acquisition and control  
- Time-lapse imaging and behavioral tracking  
- Experimental design for quantitative biology  

---

## Project 2: Sex differences in circadian behavior

This project investigates whether male and female *Nematostella vectensis* exhibit differences in circadian behavior and activity patterns under controlled light-dark cycles.

Students will learn how to:
- Quantify behavioral rhythms  
- Analyze time-series activity data  
- Connect physiological state to behavioral output  

---

## Project 3 (exploratory): Mucus function and antimicrobial activity

This project explores whether mucus produced by *Nematostella vectensis* has antimicrobial properties against a range of microbial species.

Students will investigate:
- Mucus collection and biochemical characterization  
- Basic microbiology assays  
- Host–microbe interactions in a simple animal model  

This project treats mucus not as a passive coating, but as a potentially active defense system shaping microbial ecology.
